<notes>
Assumptions and deviations (please review):

1. Late penalty (C18): not chosen. `Depot(..., late_penalty_pct=None)` is an optional keyword. While it is None, any late delivery is refused with `PENALTY_UNSET` and changes nothing. On-time deliveries work.
2. Returned perishables (C23): I implemented the explicit clause, so a return adds a lot stamped today and it is restocked. The restock-or-discard decision is still open.
3. `deliver` carries the goods and the payment. `place_order` takes an extra required `wh` argument, because a delivery has to know which warehouse receives the goods. This deviates from the listed signature. Delivery pays `unit_cost × qty` through the same receive rules (OVERDRAWN, CAPACITY, INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS).
4. `DepotError` is defined in `events.py` to avoid a circular import, and `inventory` re-exports it. It is importable from `inventory`.
5. `rounding_mode` is a required positional argument with no default. Invalid values raise `ValueError` in the constructor, not `DepotError`. Constructor argument-type errors also raise `ValueError`.
6. `vendor/rounding.py` is not used. Its `ROUND_HALF_UP` path floors `d + 0.5`, which rounds negative halves the wrong way. Rounding goes through `decimal.quantize` directly.
7. Cash and OVERDRAWN: OVERDRAWN is emitted at each `advance_day` check while cash is below 0, not only on the transition. `receive` gives OVERDRAWN while cash is below 0. Storage is the only charge that can drive cash negative.
8. Transfer cost (C11): charged to the sending side as `transfer_credit_cents_per_unit × qty`. It is refused with `INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS` if it would push cash below 0. The name "credit" is kept from the data table, though the spec says it is a cost.
9. REORDER (C14): emitted on ship and on the source side of a transfer, when stock is at or below the item's reorder level for that warehouse. It re-arms once stock is above the level. Receipts, returns, and expiry only re-arm; they never emit.
10. Expiry (C13): at `advance_day` to day N, a lot is discarded if `N - received_day > shelf_life`. EXPIRY is emitted once per (sku, wh) per day, with the total discarded quantity. Shelf life 0 (FILM) means non-perishable.
11. Storage (C19) is charged on units held during the closing day. Expiry is applied afterwards, then the overdrawn check, then the day increments.
12. Turnover (C30): units shipped via `ship`, divided by the average of closed-day holdings in the range. Transfers do not count as shipments. Returns `None` when the average is 0.
13. Undo (C27): holds one level of snapshot covering the whole depot state, including notifications. `advance_day` clears it, and `load` clears it. Calling undo a second time raises `UNDO_BLOCKED`. Read-only calls do not affect it. `receive_bulk` is one undoable unit.
14. Bulk discount (C09): applied as `price × qty × (100 − 10) / 100`, with the rounding mode applied to the result.
15. `report()` lists only (sku, wh) positions with units > 0.
16. Capacity also applies to returned goods, which is my reading since the spec is silent.
17. `transfer` between the same warehouse gives `SAME_WAREHOUSE`. This code is not in the spec. Non-positive or non-int quantities give `BAD_ARG`, also not in the spec.
18. `audit` emits AUDIT_MISMATCH in sorted (sku, wh) order. The spec does not state an order.
19. `receive_bulk` applies receipts sequentially, so each one sees the effect of the previous ones on capacity and cash.
20. `save`/`load` are an instance method on `Depot`, not a class method. `load` requires the same warehouses and capacities as construction (otherwise `BAD_SAVE`). Rounding mode and late penalty come from the constructor.
21. `sim.simulate` raises `ValueError` for bad `days`. It skips orders with a drawn quantity of 0.
22. `data.toml` is written verbatim from the spec. The depot constructor adds no items, so callers should add the BOLT, GLUE, and FILM items themselves. `money.py` reads the config values from it.
</notes>

